---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Container from '../components/Container.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="Why Offline Childhood exists, what we argue for, what we will not do, and how the site is paid for."
>
  <header class="border-b border-line bg-surface-2 py-16 sm:py-24">
    <Container width="prose">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold sm:text-5xl">Why Offline Childhood?</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-xl leading-relaxed text-ink">
        We're parents, and we're trying. Trying to get outside more, delay the smartphone, keep
        dinner screen-free, and raise kids who can think for themselves about all of it.
      </p>
    </Container>
  </header>

  <Container width="prose" class="py-14">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        We are not purists. Nobody here lives offline, and if you do, we salute you. What we think is
        that the culture has gotten badly out of hand on screen addiction, algorithmic feeds, and now
        AI, and that childhood is where that costs the most.
      </p>
      <p>
        So we argue for the analog side. More unstructured play, more paper, more time outdoors, more
        boredom. Not because technology is evil, but because an hour spent on a screen is an hour
        spent on something else, and at this age the something else matters enormously.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we teach kids how the tech works too</h2>
      <p>
        Because they are going to use it. Probably in their careers, certainly for the rest of their
        lives. A kid who does not understand how an algorithm decides what she sees, or where an AI
        model got its material, or who is paying for the free app, is not protected. She is just
        unprepared.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is what the <a href="/conversations">Conversations</a> half of this site is for. Every
        idea is written four times over, for ages 4 to 6 through 13 and up, because a four-year-old
        can absolutely understand that a person chose what she is watching. Limits buy you the years
        in which understanding gets built. You need both.
      </p>

      <h2>What we will not do</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Scare you.</strong> Fear is the easiest way to get a parent's attention and about the
        worst way to actually help. There is already plenty of it going around. Your worries here are
        reasonable, and we are not going to farm them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Overstate the research.</strong> Whether smartphones caused the rise in teenage
        anxiety is a live fight among serious researchers, and we say so on the pages where it comes
        up. The case for a more analog childhood does not need that argument, and a site that
        oversells its evidence deserves to be doubted on everything else.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Tell you that you have already blown it.</strong> Most people arrive here with
        devices in the house and habits already set. Every recommendation on this site has a first
        step that a family in exactly that position can take this week.
      </p>

      <h2>Who is it for?</h2>
      <p>
        Parents and caregivers. The advice leans younger, because that is where most of these
        decisions get made for the first time, but plenty of it works just as well with teenagers,
        and every conversation is written for older kids too. Teachers as well: they work with one
        kid at a dinner table or a class of thirty, and each one has a note on running it as a
        group.
      </p>

      <h2>Who are you?</h2>
      <p>
        This site was founded by a mom who lives outside New York City and works in technology,
        supported by a group of other parents who read drafts, argue with them, and send us things
        worth including. She also built a few AI agents to help with the research, because these
        tools can and should be used for good.
      </p>

      <h2>How is it paid for?</h2>
      <p>
        Some product links earn us a commission, and that pays for the site. It does not decide what
        goes on it. The structure is deliberate: every recommendation shows up only after a decision
        has been argued through on its own terms, and the honest answer to most of these questions is
        that you do not need to buy anything at all.{' '}
        <a href="/disclosure">Here is the full disclosure</a>, including what we refuse to take money
        for.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14 rounded-3xl border-2 border-accent bg-accent/12 p-7">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-extrabold">Tell us what's missing</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Got a product or service we should look at? Know someone we should be reading? Came here for
        something and did not find it? Please tell us.{' '}
        <a href="mailto:hello@offlinechildhood.com" class="font-semibold text-primary-text underline underline-offset-4">
          hello@offlinechildhood.com
        </a>
      </p>
      <Button href="/plan" variant="primary" class="mt-6">Build your family plan</Button>
    </div>
  </Container>
</Base>
